the traffic stretches back several  kilometers drivers wait for over eight  hours in the blazing sun on this main  road out of donetsk  it's one of the few routes still open  for civilians to reach  government-controlled areas  they have to pass through this ukrainian  military checkpoint it's a painstakingly  slow process  we want to get back home faster we want  this to be more regulated and more  appropriate so that people don't have to  wait for hours in the heat  these are routine journeys family visits  shopping for supplies not available in  conflict areas the delays cause  resentment  there's a feeling that it's being done  on purpose so that people can see what  it's like to live not in ukraine but as  they think we would live in russia  many blame the complex bureaucracy  enforced by ukrainian authorities  documents are scrutinized questions  asked belongings searched  since the conflict started it's often  been difficult for civilians to pass  from one side to the other they need a  special document for example to go  through this ukrainian checkpoint  but in the last few weeks things have  become much harder with a major road  into the nets closed due to fighting the  city is becoming more inaccessible  and just recently buses between the two  sides have also stopped running  transport authorities in donetsk say it  wasn't their decision  no one provided us with information or  official details we found out the bus  service was stopped on the day when it  actually happened they departed  according to plan but then they were  turned back  the only buses now leaving from donetsk  are to towns within rebel-held territory  and to destinations in russia  in recent days donetsk suffered fuel  shortages too with long lines at the few  gas stations which have fuel on tap  people are already getting used to it so  if we find fuel we fill the tank up as  well as a jerry can as reserved  the rebel authorities in donetsk blame  the transport problems on the ukrainian  government accusing kiev of imposing a  blockade  they say it's pushing them closer to  russia  and with supplies from ukraine cut off  supermarkets are mostly stocked with  russian goods  customers can even pay with russian  rubles  but selection is limited fresh milk is  in short supply at this store and some  shelves are empty  so people in the conflict area depend on  aid getting in but these red cross  trucks are slowed by military controls  too and as tensions continue between the  two sides transport across the line of  control is unlikely to improve  leaving civilians trapped in the middle
 

